-- title:
--   Eqs. (9.3)–(9.4) — a two-qubit Hamiltonian commutes with $S_z^{[n]}$ iff it has the form $T(z_1,z_2,z_3,z_4,h)$
-- statement:
--   Let $n$ qubits be given, let $k\neq l$ be two of them, and let $M$ be a Hermitian two-qubit operator, placed on qubits $k,l$ (identity elsewhere) as $M^{(kl)}$. Then
--   $$
--   [S_z^{[n]},M^{(kl)}]=0\iff M=T(z_1,z_2,z_3,z_4,h)=\begin{pmatrix} z_1&0&0&0\\ 0&z_2&h&0\\ 0&\bar h&z_3&0\\ 0&0&0&z_4\end{pmatrix}\ \text{for some } z_1,\dots,z_4\in\mathbb R,\ h\in\mathbb C.
--   $$
--   So the two-qubit Hamiltonians in the commutant of the weak collective decoherence algebra are exactly the combinations of $\sigma_z^{(k)}\sigma_z^{(l)}$, $\sigma_x^{(k)}\sigma_y^{(l)}-\sigma_y^{(k)}\sigma_x^{(l)}$, $\sigma_x^{(k)}\sigma_x^{(l)}+\sigma_y^{(k)}\sigma_y^{(l)}$ (eq. (9.3)), the single-qubit terms $\sigma_z^{(k)}$, $\sigma_z^{(l)}$ and the identity.
--
--   This identifies the operators available for encoded control on the weak collective decoherence DFSs; the generators of the mission are of this form.
--
--   **Formalization Note** Qubits are numbered from $0$; $M$ is indexed with qubit $k$ first.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - encoding: "the two-qubit operators which are in A" (the commutant A′ of the algebra generated by I and S_z^{[n]}) → M^{(kl)} commutes with S_z^{[n]}; reason: commuting with I is automatic, and the page's "in A" means the commutant A′ (§9.1, p.125 opens with "elements of the commutant A′"); same statement
--   - reading: (9.3) lists the two-body terms and (9.4) is "the most general Hamiltonian on two qubits" → the commuting Hermitian M are exactly the T(z₁,z₂,z₃,z₄,h) of (9.4); reason: (9.4) adds to (9.3) the σ_z single-qubit terms found to commute after (9.1) and the identity, and is the page's conclusion; both directions are asserted ("are all given by")
-- source:
--   Bacon, Decoherence, Control, and Symmetry in Quantum Computers, arXiv:quant-ph/0305025v1, §9.1.1, eqs. (9.2)–(9.4), p.125 (PDF p.142)

import Definitions.Def_BaconThesis_WeakUniv_Tmat
import Definitions.Def_BaconThesis_WeakUniv_embed
import Definitions.Def_BaconThesis_WeakUniv_Sz

namespace BaconThesis.WeakUniv

/-- **(9.3)–(9.4): the two-qubit Hamiltonians in the commutant of weak collective decoherence.**
Bacon, *Decoherence, Control, and Symmetry in Quantum Computers*, arXiv:quant-ph/0305025v1,
§9.1.1, eqs. (9.2)–(9.4), p.125 (PDF p.142): the two-qubit operators acting between the `k`th and
`l`th qubit that commute with `S_z^{[n]}` are `h₃₃σ_zσ_z + h₁₂(σ_xσ_y − σ_yσ_x) + h₁₁(σ_xσ_x + σ_yσ_y)`
(9.3) together with the local `σ_z` terms and the identity, and "The most general Hamiltonian on two
qubits i and j is then of the form T_ij(z1, z2, z3, z4, h) = [[z1,0,0,0],[0,z2,h,0],[0,h*,z3,0],
[0,0,0,z4]] (9.4)".

For `n` qubits, two distinct qubits `k ≠ l` and a Hermitian two-qubit operator `M`, the operator
`M` placed on qubits `k, l` commutes with `S_z^{[n]}` if and only if `M = T(z₁, z₂, z₃, z₄, h)` for
some real `z₁, …, z₄` and complex `h`.

**Formalization Note.** Qubits are `0, …, n−1`; `M` is indexed by `(x_k, x_l)` (qubit `k` first);
`S_z^{[n]}` is (8.33). The book's operators are Hamiltonians, hence the hypothesis that `M` is
Hermitian. -/
theorem eq_9_4 {n : ℕ} (k l : Fin n) (hkl : k ≠ l)
    (M : Matrix (Fin 2 × Fin 2) (Fin 2 × Fin 2) ℂ) (hM : M.IsHermitian) :
    Commute (Sz n) (embed n k l M) ↔
      ∃ z₁ z₂ z₃ z₄ : ℝ, ∃ h : ℂ, M = Tmat z₁ z₂ z₃ z₄ h := by sorry

end BaconThesis.WeakUniv
